# LEGACY-RME-PDF-HISTORY-1B — Doctor clinical branch context (permanent rules)

1. A doctor reads a published legacy archive only when **BOTH** gates pass:
   a canonical treating relationship (`DoctorPatientScopeService`) **AND** a
   valid clinical **branch** context. Neither gate alone ever authorizes.

2. **Same branch is never authorization.** A doctor who practises in the branch
   that owns the archive but has no treating relationship with the patient is
   refused (403 — in scope, not authorized).

3. **A treating relationship never bypasses branch isolation.** A doctor who
   treats the patient but does not practise in the archive's origin branch is
   refused (404 — outside scope, which is also the anti-enumeration boundary).

4. The canonical clinical branch context of a doctor is **server-derived** as:

   ```
   mst_doctor_branches (Cabang Praktik yang Diizinkan)
       ∩ active, RME-enabled branches
   ```

   resolved by `App\Modules\Doctor\Services\DoctorClinicalBranchResolver`. That
   pivot is the system's own source of truth — `startDoctorSession` refuses any
   branch outside it — so nothing else may redefine it.

5. **Doctors are multi-branch.** Never model a doctor's clinical authority as a
   single `users.branch_id`, a single `BranchContext` branch, or the branch of
   the current visit. Production doctors carry `users.branch_id = NULL`.

6. `BranchContext::forUser()` answers "which branch is this operator working in
   right now?" and falls back to MAIN. It is correct for operators and **wrong
   for deciding a doctor's clinical read scope**: with no online session it
   resolves to a branch the doctor has no relationship with, and MAIN is never
   RME-enabled, so a genuinely entitled doctor was denied.

7. **Fail closed, never fall back.** No doctor master link, an inactive doctor
   master, no practice branch, or an empty intersection ⇒ **empty scope ⇒ deny
   everything**. Never fall back to all branches, the patient's branch, the
   first active branch, or MAIN.

8. A branch that is deactivated or has RME switched off **drops out** of the
   set even while the assignment row survives.

9. **The online context never widens the set.** A surviving
   `trx_user_online_contexts` row pointing at a branch that is no longer in the
   doctor's practice set grants nothing: revoking a practice branch revokes the
   reads with it.

10. `origin_branch_id` remains provenance **and** a visibility scope; it is
    never an authorization bypass, and branch ids are never read from the
    request.

11. A doctor's legacy reach must stay **strictly narrower** than their native
    reach. Native access is the clinical relationship across the RME branch
    set; legacy additionally requires the origin branch to be one they practise
    in. Any future change that inverts this is a regression.

12. Legacy clinical read stays **read-only**: it never creates a ClinicVisit,
    MedicalRecord, invoice, payment, consent, odontogram, LabOrder,
    LabCaseCandidate or SATUSEHAT candidate, and never converts legacy to
    native.

13. HISTORY-1B changes **read scope only**. With the migration capability OFF
    every mutation path (upload, processing/retry, review, publish, void,
    branch admission) stays blocked, and branch admission stays a separate
    concern from a doctor's clinical branch context.

14. A doctor's practice-branch assignment is **operational configuration**, not
    a code constant: it is maintained through the doctor master (Master Data →
    Dokter, `DoctorRepository` syncing `mst_doctor_branches`). Never hardcode
    doctor ids, patient ids or branch ids into authorization.

15. Test fixtures must state the practice branch on the **doctor master**
    (`Doctor::factory()->withAllowedBranches([...])` or `branch_id`), not only
    on the user row. A fixture that sets `users.branch_id` alone no longer
    describes a doctor who works there.

16. Real production Doctor UAT (treating doctor reads their patient's published
    archive, plus a same-branch non-treating negative) is required before a
    HISTORY-1B-class GO. Automated tests alone are not sufficient evidence.
